From 6e39f936cd94d039bc49062e9c9d39ccdd024785 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 11:01:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 104/104] Hand the displaced legacy frame back through an out
 parameter

Item 4-5 attributed 92% of a call's bookkeeping to the Annex B
caller/arguments frame -- 44 ns on 60.16% of the corpus's calls -- and
item 0111 then priced the named fix, relocating the frame to a
thread-local stack, at 0.20% and refused it. Its third arm is why: one
56-byte Arguments copy alone is 8.19 ns, so the cost is the copying, and
relocating where the copying lands does not remove it. The lever it
pointed at is removing copies.

PushLegacyFrame returned a 72-byte LegacyFrame by value and the caller
assigned it. An out parameter writes it straight into the caller's local
instead, which is the same displaced frame with two fewer copies of it
and no semantic change at all.

Measured in situ with call-entry-cost, twelve interleaved ABBA pairs
against a baseline binary built from the unmodified tree on this
machine: InvokeFunction 117.32 -> 115.50 ns and the legacy frame itself
44.05 -> 42.02, out ahead on 9 of 12 pairs. That is 1.83 ns on
59 372 476 invocations -- 109 ms of a 108 879 ms driver, 0.100% -- and
9 of 12 is not the clean separation 3.5 requires to claim a win. It is
kept because it is strictly less work for identical semantics, not
because 0.100% is established.

The gap between the two numbers is the finding worth keeping. 0111
priced a struct copy at 8.19 ns and this removes two of them for 1.83,
so most of the return-by-value traffic was already elided before it
reached the machine: a struct copy in the source is not a struct copy in
the code, and a replica that counts copies in C# overstates what the JIT
actually emits.

Annex B behaviour is unchanged -- LegacyCallerArgumentsTests, 14 cases,
and 8 057 tests across six projects with no failures.
